      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115070,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 171,
        "headingId": 580,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T12:36:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 751,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 581,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115071,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 171,
        "headingId": 581,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most craniofacial CMFs occur in the jaw and sinonasal bones, but any craniofacial bone can be affected {9540086; 9596266; 12946671; 14614877; 16165913; 21544896; 26929898; 29324473}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T12:36:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 752,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 582,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115072,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 171,
        "headingId": 582,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Depending on the site, there may be tinnitus, visual disturbances, headaches, hearing loss, and sinonasal congestion {9596266; 26929898; 31754576; 32209521}.</p>\r\n<h5>Imaging</h5>\r\n<p>Radiographically, craniofacial CMFs are similar in appearance to CMFs in other locations. They are primarily lytic, often with a sclerotic rim, and they frequently demonstrate cortical thinning or erosion {9596266; 20614285}. Matrix mineralization occurs in approximately 10% of cases and is usually focal {9596266}. A subset of craniofacial CMFs develop on the surface of bone {29324473}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T12:36:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 753,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 583,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115073,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 171,
        "headingId": 583,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>CMF is rare, and 5% of all cases involve craniofacial bones {9596266; 20614285}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T12:36:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 754,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 584,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115074,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 171,
        "headingId": 584,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T12:36:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 755,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 585,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115075,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 171,
        "headingId": 585,
        "headingText": "<p>The majority of CMFs harbour a recombination of the glutamate receptor gene <em>GRM1</em> with one of several 5&prime; partner genes, which represent strong promoters, leading to high GRM1 expression. This is considered the driver event for approximately 90% of CMFs {24658000}. GRM1 expression is absent or very low in other cartilaginous tumours {24658000}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2024-01-10T12:47:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 762,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 592,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115076,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 171,
        "headingId": 592,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>CMF is a lobular, grey-white tumour with a smooth surface.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T12:36:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 756,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 586,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115077,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 171,
        "headingId": 586,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>CMF consists of a lobular proliferation of spindled and stellate cells with abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm and a chondromyxoid background {9596266}. The lobules show a hypocellular centre and a hypercellular periphery with intermingled osteoclast-like giant cells. In a third of cases there are scattered coarse calcifications, but hyaline cartilage is only occasionally seen {9596266}. Cytological atypia may occur; however, mitoses are uncommon {9596266}. In approximately 10% of cases, cystic haemorrhagic degeneration produces aneurysmal bone cyst&ndash;like changes {9596266}.</p>\r\n<h5>Immunohistochemistry</h5>\r\n<p>Immunohistochemistry is typically not required. CMF shows variable staining for SMA, S100, EMA, and SOX9 and is negative for cytokeratins and GFAP {2263591; 19801163; 29324473}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T12:36:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 757,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 587,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115078,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 171,
        "headingId": 587,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T12:36:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 758,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 588,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115079,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 171,
        "headingId": 588,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Molecular pathology is not usually needed for diagnosis. If required, upregulation of GRM1 expression can help distinguish CMF from its mimics {24658000}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T12:36:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 761,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 591,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115080,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 171,
        "headingId": 591,
        "headingText": "<p><em>Essential:</em> radiographic lytic lesion with sharp margins, a lobulated lesion; zonal distribution of spindled and stellate cells; chondromyxoid matrix; condensation of lesional cells at the periphery of lobules, admixed with multinucleated giant cells.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-11-13T09:13:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 759,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 589,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115081,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 171,
        "headingId": 589,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T12:36:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 760,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 590,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115082,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 171,
        "headingId": 590,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The prognosis is excellent, even for recurrent tumours. Recurrence is more common in craniofacial CMFs because it is challenging to obtain clear surgical margins {20614285; 25217119; 26929898; 29324473}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T12:36:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]